/*
Approach:

1. Handle the edge cases first:
   - If the list is empty or has only one node, return null.
   - In both cases, deleting the middle leaves an empty list.

2. Use three pointers:
   - slow → moves one node at a time.
   - fast → moves two nodes at a time.
   - prev → keeps track of the node before slow.

3. Move the pointers:
      prev = slow;
      slow = slow.next;
      fast = fast.next.next;

   - When fast reaches the end, slow will be pointing to the
     middle node.
   - prev will be pointing to the node just before the middle.

4. Delete the middle node:
      prev.next = slow.next;

   - Instead of directly deleting slow, skip it by connecting
     prev to slow.next.

5. Return head because the first node of the list remains unchanged.

Key Idea:
Use the slow-fast pointer technique to find the middle node,
while prev keeps track of the node before it. Once slow reaches
the middle, skip slow using prev.next.

Example:
1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5

slow = 3
prev = 2

prev.next = slow.next

Result:
1 → 2 → 4 → 5

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
*/



class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteMiddle(ListNode head) {
        if(head == null || head.next == null) {
            return null;
        }
        ListNode slow = head;
        ListNode fast = head;
        ListNode prev = null;

        while(fast != null && fast.next != null){
            prev = slow;
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }
        prev.next = slow.next;
        return head;
    }
}
